sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch07-15> a delphi:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous rules can yield discrete behavior, so discrete models often fit" ;
    dct:description "Even systems governed by continuous equations, such as certain chemical reaction kinetics, commonly have a limited number of stable states with discrete transitions between them, and because discrete overall behavior is often a system's most obvious feature, discrete models like cellular automata are often the most appropriate." ;
    delphi:context "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 7, “Mechanisms in Programs and Nature”; section “The Problem of Satisfying Constraints” (from p. 342), p. 342. Scope: natural systems with continuous underlying dynamics but discrete overall behavior, such as phase transitions and bistable chemical reactions" ;
    dct:type "model-of-nature" .
}

